{title: Come, Ye Disconsolate}
{artist: Thomas Moore, Thomas Hastings / Samuel Webbe}
{genre: Hymn}
{style: Traditional}
{flow: Meditative}
{key: C}
{tempo: 92}
{time: 4/4}
{score: come-ye-disconsolate.abc}
{c: Chords checked against the four-part harmony of the Open Hymnal score (Come_Ye_Disconsolate-Consolator_Webbe)}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[C]Come, ye disconsolate, [F]where’er [C]ye languish,
[C]Come to the [F]mercy seat, [G]fer__[D7]__vently [G]kneel.
[C]Here [Ab7]bring your [C]wounded hearts, here [F]tell your [C]anguish;
[C]Earth [E]has no [F]sorrow that [C]Heav’n__[G7]__ cannot [C]heal.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[C]Joy of the desolate, [F]light of [C]the straying,
[C]Hope of the [F]penitent, [G]fade__[D7]__less and [G]pure!
[C]Here [Ab7]speaks the [C]Comforter, ten[F]derly [C]saying,
[C]Earth [E]has no [F]sorrow that [C]Heav’n__[G7]__ cannot [C]cure.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[C]Here see the Bread of Life, [F]see wa[C]ters flowing
[C]Forth from the [F]throne of God, [G]pure__[D7]__ from a[G]bove.
[C]Come [Ab7]to the [C]feast of love; come, [F]ever [C]knowing
[C]Earth [E]has no [F]sorrow but [C]Heav’n__[G7]__ can re[C]move.
{end_of_verse}
